WEBVTT

00:00:00.581 --> 00:00:04.592
<v><b>P:</b></v> Presumably, not everyone is convinced by this research?

00:00:05.221 --> 00:00:10.727
<v><b>J:</b></v> No. The book hasn’t been well received by the free-range parent community,

00:00:11.053 --> 00:00:15.916
who argue that any kind of intensive parenting ruins family life.

00:00:16.395 --> 00:00:23.675
They say it harms children’s happiness and doesn’t produce particularly successful or creative offspring.

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:28.896
<v><b>P:</b></v> Yes, they tend to think children are hugely over-supervised these days,

00:00:29.271 --> 00:00:33.998
that they don’t have time to think for themselves and just expect to be entertained…

00:00:34.455 --> 00:00:39.858
<v><b>J:</b></v> Yes, but the authors of the book say that the evidence doesn’t really support that.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:43.562
They argue that it’s about finding a balance.

00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:46.383
The really clever part about helicopter parenting

00:00:46.408 --> 00:00:52.303
when it’s done well is that these parents use what social scientists call ‘soft power’.

00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:56.861
They are aiming to shape children’s values and preferences so

00:00:56.886 --> 00:01:00.671
that the children appear to be making those choices of their own accord.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:07.885
So, their children tend to acquire a good work ethic and resilience when they are faced with challenges

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:14.274
– and these are valuable personal characteristics that will have an effect on their future success.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.963
There’s also a Swedish study which says that these children are less susceptible to depression,

00:01:21.563 --> 00:01:25.855
better at concentrating, and generally more self-confident.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.157
<v><b>P:</b></v> Does the book imply, then, that this is the best parenting system

00:01:30.182 --> 00:01:34.031
regardless of what country you’re from or what the education system is?

00:01:34.348 --> 00:01:38.545
<v><b>J:</b></v> No, and this is, in a way, the most interesting argument.

00:01:38.996 --> 00:01:43.840
The authors don’t say that there is only one possible parenting style.

00:01:44.293 --> 00:01:50.016
What they explain is that parenting styles are a reflection of the economic situation

00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:52.693
in a particular country at a certain time.

00:01:53.881 --> 00:01:58.472
So, in countries where there is considerable economic inequality,

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:01.662
such as the UK or the US at the moment,

00:02:02.101 --> 00:02:06.186
there are more parents who push their children to succeed from a young age,

00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:09.713
because there’s so much to be gained by doing well in life.

00:02:10.214 --> 00:02:13.577
However, in the UK in the 1970s,

00:02:13.863 --> 00:02:18.317
when there was less difference between the lives of the most and least successful,

00:02:18.795 --> 00:02:23.639
parenting styles were generally more relaxed, which was appropriate at that time.

00:02:24.683 --> 00:02:29.147
And, today, if you look at countries where the average income is higher

00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:35.594
and they have less economic inequality, such as Sweden and Finland, again,

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:42.423
parenting can still be more relaxed because there’s not so much to lose by not being a high achiever.

00:02:43.248 --> 00:02:48.659
And, typically, children in Scandinavia and the Netherlands tend to be far less competitive,

00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:54.692
and qualities such as creativity and independence are more highly prized,

00:02:54.996 --> 00:02:57.460
because that’s what they need to succeed.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.711
<v><b>P:</b></v> So, what kind of parents are the authors? Do they say in the book?

00:03:02.105 --> 00:03:07.171
<v><b>J:</b></v> Both authors say they’re almost certainly helicopter parents, but,

00:03:08.577 --> 00:03:13.176
as I was saying, they don’t think that this is the only ‘right’ parenting style.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.627
<v><b>P:</b></v> Just that it produces the best outcomes in unequal societies?

00:03:17.894 --> 00:03:19.403
<v><b>J:</b></v> Yes, that’s right.

00:03:19.491 --> 00:03:25.093
And they highlight the importance of having the right parenting style for your circumstances.

00:03:25.565 --> 00:03:30.672
For example, being an over-controlling parent – a tiger parent

00:03:31.134 --> 00:03:34.797
– may produce great exam grades, but these children,

00:03:34.984 --> 00:03:39.184
often those from countries where there is a huge focus on academic achievement,

00:03:39.495 --> 00:03:44.966
have the most mental health problems, along with low levels of creativity.

00:03:45.742 --> 00:03:49.583
These children never have the opportunity to develop their natural talents,

00:03:49.975 --> 00:03:53.626
and it can make them stressed and unhappy in the short term.

00:03:54.366 --> 00:03:56.293
And, with free-range parenting,

00:03:56.487 --> 00:04:01.147
children may develop their creative side and learn to make their own decisions,

00:04:01.525 --> 00:04:04.589
but they may also make the wrong decisions,

00:04:04.988 --> 00:04:10.103
or miss opportunities because their parents aren’t involved enough.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:14.593
<v><b>P:</b></v> Well, how fascinating, and how difficult to get that balance right.

00:04:14.923 --> 00:04:20.220
If you want to read more, the book we’ve been discussing today is <i>Love, Money, and Parenting:</i>

00:04:20.486 --> 00:04:23.576
<i>How Economics Explains the Way We Raise Our Kids</i>,

00:04:23.826 --> 00:04:29.690
by Matthias Doepke and Fabrizio Zilibotti, published by Princeton University Press.

00:04:30.023 --> 00:04:32.461
Thank you very much, Janice, for talking to us today…

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.994
So, what kind of parent are you? I have to say I’m totally free-range…